+## Per-AC-A reasoning
+
+Task 1's own `**ACTION**` line scopes itself explicitly: "serves AC-A1,
+AC-A2, AC-A3, AC-A4, AC-A5." That is the exact boundary this suite honors.
+
+**AC-A1 (PRD AC-2) — read() prefers the durable store.** Genuinely testable
+pure logic. Covered: durable holds a token, legacy holds a *different* token
+(deliberately, so the case is discriminative) — read() returns the durable
+value and leaves legacy untouched. Verdict: **NEW_TEST_REQUIRED**.
+
+**AC-A2 (PRD AC-2) — read() migrates a legacy-only token exactly once.**
+Covered by three cases: (1) the base migration — legacy-only token is
+copied into durable, legacy is cleared, and the token is returned; (2) a
+*second* read is served from durable alone even after legacy is reseeded
+with a different, stale value — proving legacy is genuinely no longer
+consulted, not merely "usually" not consulted; (3) the same migration also
+fires when `durable.read()` *rejects*, not only when it resolves empty —
+the plan's rule 2 names both triggers explicitly ("If the durable store
+holds nothing — or if `durable.read()` rejects"). Verdict:
+**NEW_TEST_REQUIRED**.
+
+**AC-A3 (PRD AC-2) — migration never destroys the only copy.** Covered by
+two cases: (1) a failed durable write during migration leaves the legacy
+copy intact and still returns the token; (2) a subsequent read — no longer
+injected with a failure — completes the migration, serving the plan's own
+"the migration retries on the next read" clause. Verdict:
+**NEW_TEST_REQUIRED**.
+
+**AC-A4 (PRD AC-2) — save() degrades to the legacy store when durable is
+unavailable.** Covered: `durable.write()` rejects, `save()` still resolves
+(does not propagate the failure to the caller) and the token lands in the
+legacy store instead. Verdict: **NEW_TEST_REQUIRED**.
+
+**Task 1 rule 4 (no dedicated AC-A number) — save() writes durably and
+drops a stale legacy copy on success.** Named explicitly in Task 1's
+behaviour list ("so a value left behind in the legacy store can never later
+shadow the token the owner just pasted") but not assigned its own AC-A —
+it is the positive-path complement of AC-A4 and is required to fully
+implement the contract AC-A1 depends on (durable being authoritative).
+Covered: legacy pre-seeded with a stale token, `save()` writes durably and
+clears legacy, and a follow-up `read()` proves the new token — not the
+stale one — is what comes back. Verdict: **NEW_TEST_REQUIRED**.
+
+**AC-A5 (PRD AC-2) — clear() clears BOTH stores and never rejects.**
+Covered by four cases: (1) the base both-cleared case; (2) `clear()`
+resolves without rejecting AND still clears legacy when durable's clear
+throws; (3) the mirror case for legacy throwing; (4) the interaction case
+the plan's own Notes call "load-bearing, not defensive tidiness" — a
+pre-clear state where durable holds the current token and legacy still
+holds a stale one; after `clear()`, a `read()` must return `null`, not
+resurrect the stale legacy token through AC-A2's migration path (which is
+exactly the "token screen keeps coming back" failure AC-2 exists to
+remove). Verdict: **NEW_TEST_REQUIRED**.
+
+**Task 1 rule 7 (no dedicated AC-A number) — a blank stored value is not a
+token.** Cross-cutting: threads through both `read()` branches. Covered by
+four cases: an empty-string durable value falls back to legacy; a
+whitespace-only durable value falls back to legacy (a distinct case from
+empty-string — a naive `value === ""` check would pass the first and fail
+the second); a whitespace-only *legacy* value is not migrated when durable
+is also empty (`read()` returns `null`, nothing is copied); and the fully-
+empty baseline (`null` in both stores) returns `null` without migrating
+anything. Verdict: **NEW_TEST_REQUIRED**.
